# run-96 ruling: independent verification (coordinator)

## The deliverable exists despite a misleading report
run-96 reported "succeeded, exit code 0, 0 tokens". 0 tokens would normally mean
"did nothing" (I caught exactly that in run-98 this same round). Here it did the work:
  commit 3e2e7cde "docs(architecture): rule that build-phase diagnostics fall under the
  acceptance standard"; file docs/architecture/rulings/BUILD-PHASE-DIAGNOSTIC-RULING.md
  (233 lines); working tree clean.
=> Neither "exit 0" nor the token count settles whether work happened. Only the
   deliverable does. (PIT-335, now with a counter-example in both directions.)

## Claim 1: the standard is quoted accurately -- VERIFIED
sed -n '78p;80p' docs/specs/style/02-translator-implementation-standard.md
  :78 "... on every target: kotlinc, rustc, the TypeScript compiler, the Dart analyzer,
       and the Swift type-checker. A translation that produces a warning is an emitter
       defect with the same severity as a translation that produces wrong output."
  :80 "Acceptance for any emitter change counts the warning lines in the target suite
       output that name files under the generated trees; the count is zero. A change that
       replaces a warning with a suppression marker fails acceptance."
Both quoted verbatim and correctly. The ruling's reading -- :78 names only the type
CHECKER while the sentence's head is universal ("compiles without warnings on every
target") -- is a real internal tension in the text, not an invention.

