## Claim 3 (the decisive one): S1 zeroes the diagnostic -- VERIFIED BY ME
S1's commit message supplies evidence at dc-warn/out/p08-s1/evidence/. I counted with the
file:line:col: severity SHAPE, not `grep 'warning:'`:

  baseline-build.stderr.log   diagnostics = 1   ->  gen/gap/Gap.swift:117:9: warning:
                                                   will never be executed
  after-build.stderr.log      diagnostics = 0
  after-gap.o                 278824 B in BOTH states (object still produced)

  NOTE the trap, which I hit while checking: `grep -c 'warning:' baseline-build.stderr.log`
  returns 2, because Swift prints the caret/context line too. The correct count is 1.
  The naive count would have made the baseline look like 2 diagnostics and the
  1 -> 0 claim look wrong. Count by diagnostic shape, never by substring.

## The apparent contradiction I had to resolve (not paper over)
tests/swift-gap-boundary/gap-boundary.test.ts:58 still says the fixture "still emits one
`will never be executed` warning at Gap.swift:117" under `swiftc -c`. Read alone, that
contradicts the ruling's "zeroed" claim. It is not a live contradiction:
  - that paragraph narrates the BASELINE (pre-S1) state and records it as a KNOWN
    BASELINE FAILURE per work-plan:417 ("does not waive the standard");
  - S1's own commit message states the anti-rot pin was INVERTED to assert ZERO
    "because the count was re-read as 0".
So the comment is preserved rationale, not a claim about the present. Both artifacts
agree once the timeline is respected. Recorded here because a future reader will
otherwise hit the same apparent conflict.

## Also confirmed: the -typecheck blindness the ruling relies on
S1's message: "swiftc -typecheck reports 0 diagnostics on the BASELINE too -- it cannot
see this diagnostic and is never evidence here." This matches the earlier finding that
-typecheck is blind to Gap.swift:117, and is why the standard's wording (which names only
the type-checker) matters: read narrowly it would exempt a real emitter defect.

## My verdict
The ruling is technically sound and its two load-bearing claims reproduce. It also keeps
the standings intact (P08 c8ae0054 stays NOT PASSED / REJECTED, final; S1 cd70eb12 is
candidate material only, nominating nothing). I accept it as the ruling of record for
t-baseline-diagnostic acceptance, while noting it is a NEW ruling from a seat this round
and is therefore itself subject to management confirmation like any other.
